---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'About';
const description =
  'Five years of Android development in Java, a career in QA engineering, and now building and shipping products of my own.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      I'm Priya Kaur. I'm a QA Engineer based in Australia, and I build and ship
      my own software.
    </p>

    <h2>Android, 2014–2019</h2>

    <p>
      I started out building Android applications in Java. For five years that
      was the work: Android Studio, activities and fragments, layouts, and the
      long tail of making an app behave the same way on hardware I had never
      held. Shipping to real devices teaches you quickly that a feature working
      on your machine is not the same as a feature working.
    </p>

    <h2>QA engineering</h2>

    <p>
      Then I moved into quality. People sometimes read that as a step away from
      building. It isn't. Quality engineering is about verifying things properly
      instead of assuming they work — writing the test that would catch the
      failure rather than the one that confirms what you already believe.
    </p>

    <p>
      Most of the job is being specific before anyone claims something is done.
      What does working mean here? What state has to exist first? What happens
      on the path nobody planned for? Those questions are unglamorous and they
      are the difference between software that holds up and software that looks
      finished.
    </p>

    <h2>Building again</h2>

    <p>
      Now I'm building products of my own, with AI assistance for the parts I
      would otherwise have spent weeks getting up to speed on. The first is
      markAtt, an attendance and homework tracking app for a community Sunday
      school in Brisbane.
    </p>

    <p>
      The QA background is the advantage here, not a detour. When writing code
      stops being the slow part, verification becomes the bottleneck: knowing
      what to check, how a thing fails, and what done actually means. That is
      the part that doesn't get easier, and it's the part I have spent years on.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
